    tmk:sourceUrl "https://manuals.ipaustralia.gov.au/trademark/relevant-legislation25"^^xsd:anyURI ;
    tmk:upstreamRef "TMM/Part32B/x-relevant-legislation25" .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part33/1/1/1> a tmk:Chunk,
        tmk:ManualPassage ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> ;
    tmk:authorityKind "practice" ;
    tmk:capturedIn tmk:snapshot-c490a9927f1a ;
    tmk:chunkKind "body" ;
    tmk:citesProvision <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s164> ;
    tmk:contentHash "sha256:5bc6849712bfb0ec41743f787c529878a1586cfac7c6be43a80ce94c569bf76d" ;
    tmk:hasCitation <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part33/1/1/1/prov/0> ;
    tmk:headingPath "1.1",
        "33.1. What is a collective trademark?",
        "Part 33 Collective Trade Marks" ;
    tmk:onPage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part33/1> ;
    tmk:ordinal 2 ;
    tmk:partId "Part33" ;
    tmk:text "Section 164 states: An application for the registration of a collective trade mark must be made by the association to which the mark belongs." ;
    tmk:upstreamRef "TMM/Part33/1/1/1" .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part33/2/2/1> a tmk:Chunk,
        tmk:ManualPassage ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> ;
    tmk:authorityKind "practice" ;
    tmk:capturedIn tmk:snapshot-c490a9927f1a ;
    tmk:chunkKind "body" ;
    tmk:citesProvision <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s163(1)> ;
    tmk:contentHash "sha256:688c9dc43ead1194846f433e00ab73145607477534f8eb48a85e6792c0ef6226" ;
    tmk:hasCitation <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part33/2/2/1/prov/0> ;
    tmk:headingPath "2.1",
        "33.2. Application of Act",
        "Part 33 Collective Trade Marks" ;
    tmk:onPage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part33/2> ;
    tmk:ordinal 2 ;
    tmk:partId "Part33" ;
    tmk:text "Section 163(1) of the Act provides that except for Part 10 of the Act (Assignment and Transmission of Trade Marks), a collective trade mark is to be considered in the equivalent manner to an ordinary trade mark. A reference in the Act to a trade mark registered by a person is taken to include a reference to a trade mark registered by an association." ;
    tmk:upstreamRef "TMM/Part33/2/2/1" .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part33/2/2/2> a tmk:Chunk,
        tmk:ManualPassage ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> ;
    tmk:authorityKind "practice" ;
    tmk:capturedIn tmk:snapshot-c490a9927f1a ;
    tmk:chunkKind "body" ;
    tmk:citesProvision <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s163(2)> ;
    tmk:contentHash "sha256:c6bf214cf5536be84b36527d409fee0d84e352c9de6b26440c6085a49b09bb11" ;
    tmk:hasCitation <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part33/2/2/2/prov/0> ;
    tmk:headingPath "2.2",
        "33.2. Application of Act",
        "Part 33 Collective Trade Marks" ;
    tmk:onPage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part33/2> ;
    tmk:ordinal 3 ;
    tmk:partId "Part33" ;
    tmk:text "Under section 163(2) use by any one or more members of the association is use for the purposes of the Act." ;
    tmk:upstreamRef "TMM/Part33/2/2/2" .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part34/6/6/1~2> a tmk:Chunk,
        tmk:ManualPassage ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> ;
    tmk:authorityKind "practice" ;
    tmk:capturedIn tmk:snapshot-c490a9927f1a ;
    tmk:chunkKind "body" ;
    tmk:citesCase <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/CASE/1995/ATMO/39> ;
    tmk:contentHash "sha256:67f27d53e078d6fba78546aa51b71b594cf434b861d123e8f6addd38d763c414" ;
    tmk:hasCitation <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part34/6/6/1~2/case/0> ;
    tmk:headingPath "6.1 Practice under the Trade Marks Act 1955",
        "Part 34 Defensive Trade Marks",
        "Part 34.6. Grounds for rejecting a defensive application under section 187" ;
    tmk:onPage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part34/6> ;
    tmk:ordinal 3 ;
    tmk:partId "Part34" ;
    tmk:text "In a more recent decision of the Registrar under the 1955 Act (Re Mobil Oil Corporation [1995] ATMO 39), Hearing Officer Williams applied the principles of the Ferodo case but allowed for the differences between the Australian and United Kingdom legislation. The trade mark for which defensive registration was sought, in a number of classes of goods and services, was the word mark Mobil limited to the colours blue and red with the letter O in red and the remaining letters in blue. While the applicant was required to restrict the very broad specifications as filed, the applications were allowed to proceed for goods and services which were not related to the class 4 goods and class 42 services for which the Part A trade marks were registered. The hearing officer took into account the special manner of rendition of the trade mark and the fact that the applicant’s business and trading interests were widely known and had been diversified to include convenience stores. He applied the principle stated by Evershed J in the Ferodo case that if the trade mark had been registered and used in respect of a wide variety of goods required by the common man, its application to other goods of even a very different kind might fairly lead to the inference of a trade connection." ;
    tmk:upstreamRef "TMM/Part34/6/6/1~2" .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part34/6/6/2> a tmk:Chunk,
        tmk:ManualPassage ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> ;
    tmk:authorityKind "practice" ;
    tmk:capturedIn tmk:snapshot-c490a9927f1a ;
    tmk:chunkKind "body" ;
    tmk:citesProvision <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s187> ;
    tmk:contentHash "sha256:92fc3a53d56ccb61c5337dd5694f480ce1968d6c87f1b7b7d5f5e4f692f9ddf3" ;
    tmk:hasCitation <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part34/6/6/2/prov/0> ;
    tmk:headingPath "6.2 Factors to consider in applying section 187",
        "Part 34 Defensive Trade Marks",
        "Part 34.6. Grounds for rejecting a defensive application under section 187" ;
    tmk:onPage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part34/6> ;
    tmk:ordinal 4 ;
    tmk:partId "Part34" ;
    tmk:text "A ground for rejection under section 187 may apply if the Registrar is satisfied that use of the trade mark on the goods or services applied for in the defensive registration is not likely to indicate a connection between the goods or services and the applicant. This decision would depend upon the evidence provided by the applicant in support of the defensive trade mark and the nature of the connection of the goods and services to the applicant. The likelihood that the necessary connection will be inferred will be influenced by a number of factors, including the following:" ;
    tmk:upstreamRef "TMM/Part34/6/6/2" .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part34/6/6/2/1> a tmk:Chunk,
        tmk:ManualPassage ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> ;
    tmk:authorityKind "practice" ;
    tmk:capturedIn tmk:snapshot-c490a9927f1a ;
    tmk:chunkKind "body" ;
    tmk:citesProvision <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMA1995/s41> ;
    tmk:contentHash "sha256:4c8b903ecc8a1f411e1a89cacd61d61bd61018c4973f20600a82ac3b063373b8" ;
    tmk:hasCitation <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/cite/TMM/Part34/6/6/2/1/prov/0> ;
    tmk:headingPath "6.2 Factors to consider in applying section 187",
        "6.2.1 The nature of the trade mark",
        "Part 34 Defensive Trade Marks",
        "Part 34.6. Grounds for rejecting a defensive application under section 187" ;
    tmk:onPage <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part34/6> ;
    tmk:ordinal 5 ;
    tmk:partId "Part34" ;
    tmk:text "Section 41 of the Act does not apply to defensive trade marks and there is no reference in Part 17 to any inherent characteristic which a defensive trade mark must possess in order to qualify for registration. The only explicitly stated requirement is that it must already have been registered and therefore it must have been found to be capable of distinguishing the goods or services for which it is already registered. A trade mark which consists of unusual or invented (or otherwise inherently distinctive)material, that is widely known for use on a specified range of goods or services is more likely to be connected with other goods or services in the mind of the consumer than a trade mark which lacks inherent adaptation. The owner of a trade mark that is not considered to be inherently adapted to distinguish is likely to have to submit much more substantial evidence to establish that connection. In addition, a trade mark may have qualified for registration in respect of the goods on which it has been used as a trade mark but have no inherent capacity to distinguish the goods or services specified in the defensive registration. For example, if the trade mark cappuccino was registered in respect of ‘carpets and rugs’ in Class 27 (or any other goods for which the mark had no meaning), no amount of use by the applicant could support a defensive registration in respect of ‘beverages’ in Class 30." ;
    tmk:upstreamRef "TMM/Part34/6/6/2/1" .

<https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ref/TMM/Part34/8> a tmk:Page ;
    rdfs:label "8. Rights given by defensive registration"@en-AU ;
    dcterms:isPartOf <https://data.ipaustralia.gov.au/tmk/ns/doc/TMM> ;
    tmk:authorityKind "practice" ;
